Z||000007||Cooter||10-15-2001||12:19 AM||||In order of least to more expensive:<BR>First thing- get rid of all the governor stuff. ESPECIALLY the parts inside the engine- there is a plastic gear and a metal washer on the crankcase cover. The weights on the gear may come apart if you just bypass the governor externally and if you don't get the washer off with the plastic gear, it will come out inside the engine and cause bad problems.<BR>Advance the ignition timing with offset flywheel keys. Just don't go overboard or you will find out how hard a Briggs can rip the jerkstarter from your hands.<BR>Get an adjustable carb- the new ones are good for a tiller, but not performance. <BR>A tuned, step bore headder.<BR>A mild "DynoCams" camshaft. Once again, don't get too carried away. Look for their "hot stock" or "cheater" cams. They will really wake a stock engine up. If you get too radical, your engine will not perform because a big cam is for an alcohol/aftermarket carb modified engine that turns up around 10,000 rpms. With a stock connecting rod, you are pushing your luck with anything over 6,000 rpms. <BR>Sooooo, an aftermarket rod and piston are a good upgrade. A long rod/short piston skirt setup with low tension rings keeps things together.<BR>That's about all that I can think of right now. I have some stuff in the storage building- cams, blocks, complete carts etc... Please come buy all of it!||||reg||1
